<article class="doc-page">
    <h1>C# union types, and how Result&lt;T&gt; became one</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        Union types are one of the longest-standing requests in C#'s history. F# has had them from the
        start; C# developers have spent years imitating them with base classes, visitor patterns and
        <code>Match</code> methods. With C# 15 and .NET 11 they are finally part of the language — and
        from version 2.5, QuickResult's <code>Result&lt;T&gt;</code> is one.
    </p>

    <h2>What a union type is</h2>
    <p>
        A union type holds a single value that is one of a fixed set of types. The types don't need a
        common base class; the union is what groups them. Because the set is closed, the compiler knows
        every possible case and can check that you handled all of them.
    </p>
    <p>C# 15 adds a one-line declaration for it:</p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@DeclarationSnippet" />
    <p>
        You read a union by switching on its <em>contents</em>. Each arm names one of the case types,
        and the switch is exhaustive: remove an arm and the compiler tells you which case is no longer
        covered.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@ExhaustiveSwitchSnippet" />
    <p>
        That last point is the one that matters. When a case is added to the union later, every switch
        that reads it is flagged until it handles the new case. With a class hierarchy and a
        <code>default</code> arm, the same change compiles silently and fails at run time.
    </p>

    <h2>A Result is the textbook union</h2>
    <p>
        "This operation produced a value, or it produced an error" is exactly a union of two cases. It's
        the example Microsoft's own union tutorial uses. Until now a C# result type had to simulate the
        union: store both a value and an error, expose an <code>IsSuccess</code> flag, and trust callers
        to check the flag before reading the right property — or offer a <code>Match</code> method that
        takes one lambda per case.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>Match</code> does force both cases to be handled, which is why QuickResult has always had
        it. But it is a library convention, not a language feature. You can't add an arm for a specific
        error type, you can't use it in an <code>is</code> expression, and the compiler's pattern
        matching knows nothing about it.
    </p>

    <h2>Result&lt;T&gt; is a union of T and IError</h2>
    <p>
        On .NET 10 and later, <code>Result&lt;T&gt;</code> is a C# union whose two cases are the success
        value <code>T</code> and the failure <code>IError</code>. You can switch on a result directly:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@ResultSwitchSnippet" />
    <p>
        Because the failure case is an interface, your own error types get their own arms. The compiler
        still requires the general <code>IError</code> arm, so an error type you didn't anticipate
        can't slip through:
    </p>
