    <h2>Return a value or an error directly</h2>
    <p>
        Each case type of a union converts to the union implicitly. For a result that means a method can
        simply return the value or the error, with no <code>Result.Success(...)</code> or
        <code>Result.Failure(...)</code> wrapper:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@ConversionSnippet" />
    <p>
        The factory methods are still there and still the clearest choice when the types alone would be
        ambiguous — see the edge cases below.
    </p>

    <h2>How QuickResult implements it</h2>
    <p>
        The one-line <code>union</code> declaration is shorthand: it generates a new struct that stores
        its contents in a single <code>object?</code> field. That is ideal for a new type, but it didn't
        fit an existing one. <code>Result&lt;T&gt;</code> is a class with an established API —
        <code>Map</code>, <code>Bind</code>, <code>Match</code>, LINQ query support. A declared union
        would have been a different type, a struct that couldn't keep the class's fields, and it
        would have boxed every <code>int</code> stored in a <code>Result&lt;int&gt;</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        C# 15 has a second route for exactly this situation: a <em>custom union type</em>. Any class or
        struct marked with <code>[Union]</code> that exposes a small set of members gets the same
        language support as a declared union. QuickResult uses it in three steps.
    </p>

    <h3>1. Mark the existing class as a union</h3>
    <p>
        <code>Result&lt;T&gt;</code> gains the <code>[Union]</code> attribute and keeps every existing
        member, so calls to <code>Map</code>, <code>Bind</code>, <code>Match</code> and the rest are
        source-compatible. The one thing that changes for C# 15 callers is how type patterns read a
        result, which is covered in the edge cases below.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Describe the cases through a member provider</h3>
    <p>
        The compiler needs to know the case types and how to read them. Rather than add those members
        to <code>Result&lt;T&gt;</code>'s public surface, QuickResult declares them on a nested interface
        named <code>IUnionMembers</code>, which the language recognises as a <em>union member
        provider</em>, and implements that interface explicitly:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@ImplementationSnippet" />
    <p>
        The two <code>Create</code> methods tell the compiler what the cases are (<code>T</code> and
        <code>IError</code>) and give it the implicit conversions. The two <code>TryGetValue</code>
        overloads are how a <code>switch</code> arm reads the contents with its own type, so matching a
        <code>Result&lt;int&gt;</code> never boxes the integer. <code>IUnionMembers</code> is public
        because the language requires it, but it is compiler infrastructure, not something to call.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Work on .NET 10 as well as .NET 11</h3>
    <p>
        <code>UnionAttribute</code> ships in .NET 11, but the compiler recognises it by name. The .NET 10
        build of QuickResult declares its own internal copy, so a project that targets .NET 10 gets
        union behaviour as long as it is compiled with the C# 15 compiler.
    </p>

    <h2>What you need to use it</h2>
    <table class="api-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Your project targets</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>.NET 11</td><td>Nothing. C# 15 is the default language version.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>.NET 10</td><td>Build with the .NET 11 SDK and set <code>&lt;LangVersion&gt;15&lt;/LangVersion&gt;</code> (or <code>latest</code>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>.NET Framework, .NET 8, .NET 9</td><td>These use the .NET Standard 2.0 build, which is not a union. Everything else — <code>Match</code>, <code>Map</code>, <code>Bind</code>, LINQ — works the same.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <CodeBlock Code="dotnet add package QuickResult" />

    <h2>Edge cases worth knowing</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Null success values.</strong> A successful result holding <code>null</code> matches
            neither arm, so add a <code>null</code> arm when <code>T</code> is nullable. For the same
            reason <code>result is null</code> is true for such a result; use <code>IsSuccess</code> to
            tell a null success from anything else.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>When T overlaps IError.</strong> If <code>T</code> is <code>IError</code>,
            <code>object</code> or an error type, a type pattern can't tell success from failure: an
            <code>IError</code> arm also matches a successful value that happens to be an error, and
            a <code>T</code> arm also matches a failure whose error is a <code>T</code>. Use
            <code>IsSuccess</code> or <code>Match</code> for those results. Implicit conversion
            follows the same overlap: an error assigned to a <code>Result&lt;object&gt;</code> becomes
            a failure, and assigning one to a <code>Result&lt;IError&gt;</code> does not compile, so
            create those with the explicit <code>Success(...)</code> and <code>Failure(...)</code>
            factories.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Patterns now look inside the result.</strong> Under C# 15,
            <code>result is Result&lt;int&gt;</code> no longer compiles when <code>result</code> is
            already a <code>Result&lt;int&gt;</code>, because the pattern tests the contents. Matching
            from <code>object</code> (<code>obj is Result&lt;int&gt; r</code>) and property patterns
            such as <code>result is { IsSuccess: true }</code> are unaffected.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Match, switch, or LINQ?</h2>
    <p>
        Nothing was taken away, so use whichever reads best:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong><code>switch</code></strong> when you are consuming a result at the edge of your code and want arms for specific error types.</li>
        <li><strong><code>Match</code></strong> when you want one expression that also works on older target frameworks, or an async version (<code>MatchAsync</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>LINQ query syntax</strong> when you are composing several steps and want the first failure to short-circuit the rest — including steps that mix sync and async.</li>
    </ul>

